<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palace of Malia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1900–1450 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century-bce">20th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Palace of Malia is a Minoan palace and settlement site 3 kilometres east of the town of Malia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Dating from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-bronze-age">Middle Bronze Age</a>, the palace was destroyed by an earthquake and rebuilt, and most of the visible ruins belong to the rebuilding. With about 7,500 square metres of floor around a central court of 48 by 23 metres, it is smaller and less elaborate than the palaces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phaistos">Phaistos</a>. Joseph Hadzidakis uncovered it in 1915, and the French School at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> excavated it from 1922.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palace-of-malia.jpg" alt="Palace of Malia" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Olaf Tausch · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mallia_Pfeilerkrypta_16.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
